{
  "markdown_report": "### Insights\n\n**Starting point.** (1) Electronics’ sampled negative-wins rate is 9.7%, versus 1.32% for CDs_and_Vinyl. This supports category-specific investigation, not validation conclusions. (2) Embedding scores near 0.5 indicate approximately zero mean cosine. Collapse regularization lacks supporting evidence. (3) Feedback suggests older interests may distract from recent behavior. Prefix removal tests this hypothesis without inferring ownership, compatibility requirements, or purchase readiness.\n\n**Related work.** (1) *Does It Look Sequential?* motivates separating recency from order sensitivity [1]. Retaining chronological suffixes preserves that distinction. (2) Recency sampling changes target exposure [2]; CL4SRec adds contrastive supervision [4]. Neither establishes a suffix-length optimum. (3) Sampled rankings and adaptive validation selection have distinct limitations [3,6]. Training margins remain diagnostic; fixed host macro HR@10 selects the condition.\n\n### Directions and framework\n\nThree directions remain: context intervention, supervision control, and ranking measurement.\n\n| Condition | Recurrent context | Supervised positions |\n|---|---|---|\n| A: baseline | Full | All valid |\n| B: matched control | Full | Retained suffix |\n| C: prefix removal | Retained suffix | Same as B |\n\nC−B estimates the training-policy effect of removing context under matched supervision; B−A estimates supervision-policy effects. Neither identifies a psychological mechanism.\n\n### Candidate ideas\n\nScores are originality/potential/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n- **Matched prefix removal: 4/7/4.** Originality: useful controlled combination, although cropping is established. Potential: reusable context experiment, although benefits remain unmeasured. Difficulty: small masks, although candidate matching and padding require care [1,2,4,5].\n- **Old-prefix shuffling: 4/6/3.** Originality: isolates older order, although follows existing perturbations. Potential: separates order from recency, although histories may become unrealistic. Difficulty: straightforward permutations, although supervision must remain matched [1].\n- **Jointly learned recurrent decay: 5/7/6.** Originality: integrates recency within one GRU, although decay gates are established. Potential: adaptive retention, although sparse histories limit identification. Difficulty: compact parameters, although architecture/predictor synchronization adds debugging.\n\n### Selected idea: matched prefix removal\n\nAt 20% progress, retain the first idea. Lower its originality/potential ratings and raise difficulty to reflect established augmentation and implementation controls.\n\n```python\nsequence, positive, negative = sample_batch(...)\nkeep = suffix_mask(sequence, p=0.5, alpha=0.5, rng=crop_rng)\ncontext = sequence * keep if condition == 'C' else sequence\nsupervise = valid if condition == 'A' else valid & keep\nfeatures = model.log2feats(context)\nloss = baseline_loss(features, positive, negative, supervise)\n```\n\nFor n real inputs, retain n with probability 1−p; otherwise draw K uniformly from ceil(αn)…n. Keep the last K inputs and aligned original targets. Single-item histories remain unchanged. This policy is a hypothesis; freeze it without tuning.\n\nMatch B/C initialization, batches, negatives, masks, candidate handling, loss semantics, regularization and reduction. Reset recurrent state per example; skip padding updates and preserve retained-step gradients [5]. Verify padding invariance, batched/single equivalence, state isolation and finite nonzero gradients. Read the final nonpadding position, never count−1. Inference uses complete permitted histories.\n\nPredeclare twelve category-condition runs, a common affordable epoch count ≤200, 200 batches/epoch, final-checkpoint selection and A→B→C tie-breaking. Preserve seeds, routing, predictor consistency and diagnostic interfaces. Track all GPU expenditure within 43,200 seconds [8]. Select one complete condition by macro validation HR@10; deploy one checkpoint/category without fusion. Report paired hit/miss changes and within-category paired-bootstrap macro intervals when available; these exclude training randomness, candidate variability and selection bias [6,7]. No improvement is claimed before evaluation.\n\nReferences: [1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12008), [2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643), [3](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/), [4](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.14395), [5](https://hidasi.eu/assets/pdf/gru4rec_v2_cikm18.pdf), [6](https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html), [7](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.03098), [8](https://aclanthology.org/D19-1224/).",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Evaluate stochastic old-prefix removal in corrected GRU4Rec through three separate conditions: unchanged baseline A, full-context training with suffix-only supervision B, and cropped-context training with identical suffix supervision C. Freeze one explicit cropping policy, compare complete conditions using four-category macro validation HR@10, and deploy exactly one independently trained checkpoint per category from the selected condition.",
    "motivation": "Qualitative feedback suggests older interactions may distract from recent interests, but sampled training margins do not establish that mechanism or validation ranking quality. Matching supervised targets and candidates separates the context-removal training policy from supervision allocation. At 20% research progress, this controlled experiment is more practical than adding learned recurrent decay. Cropping may also regularize the model rather than specifically remove stale interests; improved HR@10 alone cannot distinguish these explanations.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve the supplied corrected GRU4Rec backbone, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs, 200 batches per epoch, all four datasets, splits, host evaluator and DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']. Write all artifacts beneath the trial directory. Do not inspect other trials, reuse checkpoints, fetch pretrained weights, access hidden tests or modify host evaluation. Each run uses one GPU; four concurrent slots do not multiply the shared 43,200 GPU-second allowance.\n\nBefore examining condition results, declare twelve category-condition runs, one common epoch count E<=200 affordable within the remaining budget, and final-epoch checkpoint selection. Determine feasibility using host-provided timing or a charged baseline timing measurement; count every attempted run, failure, diagnostic and evaluation. Keep baseline optimizer, loss and other hyperparameters unchanged. If the complete comparison cannot fit, report the limitation rather than making an unmatched comparison. Use exact macro HR@10 values for selection; break equal scores by A, then B, then C. Do not tune p or alpha in this experiment. Log per-condition expenditure and best-so-far macro HR@10 against cumulative GPU-seconds only when complete four-category scores exist.\n\nFor n nonpadding inputs, retain all n with probability 0.5; otherwise sample integer K uniformly from max(1,ceil(0.5*n)) through n, inclusive. Retain the last K nonpadding positions without reordering, preserving their original aligned positive and negative IDs. Empty rows contribute no supervised loss; single-item histories are unchanged. The inclusive upper bound means the realized probability of removing context is below 0.5. Masking earlier losses in B leaves their inputs and gradient paths available to later retained targets; C removes those earlier inputs. Both retain target adjacency for every supervised position. Use full permitted histories at inference, with no cropping.\n\nUse dedicated deterministic generators for crop masks, base batches and negative sampling; initialize these identically across conditions and categories as appropriate to the supplied seed-42 baseline. Do not let crop draws alter model/dropout or base-sampling RNG streams. Match B/C initial parameters, base samples, target frequencies, negative IDs/counts/distribution, duplicate and accidental-positive handling, score activation, temperature, sampling corrections, regularization, optimizer/scheduler steps and loss denominator. Inspect the actual supplied loss and sampler during implementation: do not replace them with presumed BPR, BPR-max or cross-entropy semantics. If negatives are pooled from supervised targets, construct the same pool in B/C. Preserve A's reduction exactly; define B/C reductions identically, preferably averaging valid supervised positions if compatible with the supplied objective. Document any difference from A. Equal targets and updates do not imply equal gradient magnitudes or optimization trajectories.\n\nZero embeddings do not prevent GRU padding updates. Preserve correct baseline padding handling; otherwise compact real tokens and right-pad internally before packing, or mask every recurrent layer's state update. If a correction is necessary, apply it uniformly before the comparison and synchronize the predictor. Reset state for every independent example and preserve gradients through every retained step. Restore aligned B x L x H outputs for log2feats. Read the final nonpadding position using the maximum valid index, never count-1; handle empty histories according to the supplied baseline. Check extra-left-padding score invariance, batched versus unpadded single-example equivalence, no cross-example state leakage, finite nonzero gradients on retained embeddings and recurrent parameters, and checkpoint/predictor score agreement.\n\nPreserve item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), ModelView and view.model as the actual trained nn.Module. Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis); return measured metrics and metric_definitions, preserve model mode and handle batches (None, sequence, positive, negative). Use fixed diagnostic snapshots and candidate pairs where available, retaining padding/context checks, measurement counts, history-length strata and sampled-margin limitations. Metadata categories, ratings, prices, stores, review text and simulator traits remain unavailable. Public histories, titles, descriptions and timestamps do not establish ownership, purchase readiness or compatibility requirements. Generate full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for twenty real training users, five per category, as required; these do not replace host validation.\n\nKeep the pinned host evaluator unchanged: 200 validation users/category, 99 unique negatives and evaluation seed 42. When host outputs permit, report positive ranks, rank-8–12 cases, paired hit/miss transitions and target-versus-tenth-negative margins using the host's tie convention. Bootstrap paired users within each category, using the same resampled indices across conditions and equal category weights, for 10,000 draws with an analysis-only seed-42 RNG; report percentile intervals for category and macro HR@10 differences. Such intervals condition on fitted checkpoints and candidates; they exclude training-seed variability, candidate-sampling variability and adaptive selection bias. Three condition-level observations cannot establish diagnostic predictive validity. Update the embedded predictor writer whenever architecture or padding behavior changes. Deploy exactly one checkpoint per category from the selected condition; never average, fuse, stack or vote across conditions.",
    "pseudocode": "Predeclare A/B/C, p=0.5, alpha=0.5, common E<=200, final-checkpoint selection,\n200 batches/epoch, macro selection and tie order A then B then C.\nConfirm the complete twelve-run plan fits the remaining charged GPU budget.\n\nFor condition in [A, B, C]:\n  For category in the four required datasets:\n    initialize corrected GRU4Rec and baseline RNG streams with seed 42\n    initialize identical dedicated sampling, negative and crop generators\n    for epoch in range(E):\n      for update in range(200):\n        _, sequence, positive, negative = family_specific_sample_batch(...)\n        valid = baseline_valid_supervision_mask(sequence, positive, negative)\n        keep = zeros_like(sequence, dtype=bool)\n        for row with n > 0 nonpadding inputs:\n          K = n\n          if crop_rng.uniform() < 0.5:\n            K = crop_rng.randint_inclusive(max(1, ceil(0.5*n)), n)\n          keep[row, last_K_nonpadding_positions] = True\n        context = sequence * keep if condition == C else sequence\n        supervision = valid if condition == A else valid & keep\n        candidates = baseline_candidates_with_B_C_matching(...)\n        reset per-example recurrent state\n        features = model.log2feats(context)  # skip padded updates; aligned B x L x H\n        loss = unchanged_baseline_objective(\n            features, positive, candidates, supervision,\n            documented_reduction_identical_for_B_and_C)\n        update this model using the fixed baseline schedule\n    save final checkpoint and consistent predictor artifacts in trial directory\n    run charged family-compatible checks and diagnostics\n  obtain unchanged host macro validation HR@10 for this complete condition\n  record cumulative charged GPU-seconds\n\nSelect one condition by macro validation HR@10, breaking exact ties A then B then C.\nIf paired validation outputs are available, report category and macro paired-bootstrap\nintervals conditional on checkpoints and candidates; do not claim seed robustness.\nDeploy only the selected condition's four category checkpoints.\nAt prediction, load exactly one model/category and process full permitted history,\nreading the final nonpadding feature with the same padding semantics as training.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The matched-supervision control makes a familiar sequence augmentation more interpretable by separating context removal from target allocation.",
      "negative": "Suffix cropping, recency-focused sampling and controlled ablations are established; this is a task-specific experimental design rather than a new model class."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "A reproducible comparison can guide whether subsequent GRU improvements should address context retention or supervision allocation.",
      "negative": "Benefits remain unmeasured, and one-seed results cannot establish robustness or distinguish stale-context removal from generic regularization."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The core change uses input and supervision masks while retaining the GRU architecture and ordinary inference.",
      "negative": "Correct candidate matching, recurrent padding, RNG separation, loss reductions and budget-controlled execution require more care than a simple cropping implementation."
    }
  },
